WEBVTT

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:38.320
 This is a special midsummer episode of Delicious Revolution. In two weeks, we'll launch our fifth season of the show. It's all about migrations of all kinds. As always, we've been speaking to visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists. This season, we'll hear about food and the experience of leaving home and finding new homes, of decolonizing food traditions, and tracing recipes to the movements of diaspora. Be sure to subscribe to the podcast. You can do that on Apple Podcasts, Google Play, Stitcher, or anywhere you get your podcasts.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:01:08.920
 Just search for Delicious Revolution, and you can find all of our episodes at deliciousrevolutionshow.com. Until then, we're bringing you an interview about blackberries with Peter Buckley. Peter Buckley is a blackberry grower, philanthropist, the co-owner of Front Porch Farm. Peter's had several careers. After closing his law practice in San Francisco, he moved to India to establish a buying agency. Later, he did the same in Argentina and Brazil. Later, unusual circumstances, luck, and friendship resulted in him owning Esprit, a fashion business headquartered in Germany.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:19.840
 After meeting Mimi and having two boys in Germany, Peter decided it was time to return to San Francisco. He sold his interests in Esprit, moved to Mill Valley, and together he and Mimi built the Greenwood School.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:30.630
 Peter's interest in education led to co-founding the Center for Equal Literacy in Berkeley, and his commitment to conservation led to building the David Brower Center, also in Berkeley, as a home for the environmental community.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:55.430
 After meeting Mimi, Peter continued to work with his former business partner Doug Thompson's building national parks in Chile and supporting many forms of environmental activism. Embracing the regenerative nature of farming, Peter and Mimi started building farms in Oregon and California. Riverbend Farm and Looking Gloss Farms are organic blueberry operations in Oregon, and Front Porch Farm in Healdsburg is the highly diversified farm where Peter and Mimi are currently with. Recently, he seems to have taken a great interest in blackberries.

00:01:55.430 --> 00:02:01.960
 In this special summer episode, I talk with Peter about blackberries and the challenges and beauty of running a diverse farm.

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:10.690
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, and thanks for making some time to talk to me.

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:11.290
 Sure.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:16.020
 Can you describe Front Porch Farm for us just a little bit?

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:29.650
 Sure. Front Porch Farm is 110 acres. It lies along the Russian River. It's a little pocket valley, so we're isolated. We don't really see any neighbors, so it's quite beautiful.

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:39.650
 Of the 110 acres, about 60 of those acres are alluvial bottomlands that we grow fruit trees on, berries, lots of produce.

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:48.610
 Lots of flowers. And we also have room for field crops, alfalfa, wheat, flint corn, and some pasture.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:03:05.390
 The farm was a vineyard when we raised some eyebrows, but interestingly, practically everyone we talked to, they said, that's wonderful.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:12.290
 There's, I think people, they were happy to see a farm, to see a vineyard go back to being a farm.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:18.980
 I think everyone responds to the diversity that this particular farm represents, but diversity in general.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:26.630
 Yeah, there's a few farms where, in the midst of the monocultures of grapes, there's this explosions of biodiversity.

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:29.920
 Did you have that vision when you started this?

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:42.940
 Yes. It took us quite a few years to find a farm, and the reason it took us so long is we did have a vision of a small farm that was as diverse as we could make it.

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:50.310
 It was actually, we started around 2008 looking for a farm, and it started for the wrong reason.

00:03:50.450 --> 00:04:01.050
 It started because I was nervous about the financial markets, and up to that date, you know, I make my living as an investor.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:11.960
 And the financial markets were making me nervous, and I wanted to change things around and have assets that were real assets you could walk around on.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:18.970
 And farming seemed like something, you know, my wife, Meem, and I had always been drawn to in any case.

00:04:19.270 --> 00:04:29.340
 And we'd done quite a bit of gardening and building school gardens through our foundation, the Center for Ecoliteracy, so it wasn't strange territory for us.

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:39.980
 But how to make a living from farming and having a farm, we pretty soon realized there's different, they're very different things.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:49.020
 And so along the way, we ended up building blueberry farms up in Oregon that are, they're organic blueberry farms, but they're very much a business.

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:56.170
 And Front Porch Farm is more the farm where we can experiment and where it's hands-on.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:05:12.490
 We're doing, you know, we're not, certainly not doing all the work, but, you know, we're engaged in all the different things it takes to make the farm work from, you know, planning and prepping and cultivating to having the opportunity to talk to customers at the farmer's market.

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:22.560
 Well, that's interesting that a farm being definitely more tangible, but farming is a famously risky way to make a living, too.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:24.200
 Do you, I guess...

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:25.340
 It's not only that.

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:35.680
 What you realize very, very quickly is that, at least in farming, diversity is the enemy of profitability.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:44.170
 And you see that so vividly with our blueberry business that we, you know, we farm about 800 acres of blueberries.

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:53.200
 And we can afford to have an agronomist on staff that doesn't think about anything else but plant nutrition, diseases, and pests.

00:05:53.790 --> 00:06:00.620
 And he's out there every day testing and doing sampling and, you know, he's got it dialed in.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:03.310
 Because we have one crop, we can do that.

00:06:03.470 --> 00:06:11.440
 With Front Porch Farm, we have, I don't, I haven't counted them, but 60 or 70 crops at least, if not double that.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:14.980
 It's probably double that when you start thinking about all the different flowers that we grow.

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:23.520
 And each one of those crops has its own special kind of genius it takes to make it successful.

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:26.920
 And I think we do a good job.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:29.760
 That's a lot of genius.

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:33.000
 I'm not sure we cut all the bases covered, you know.

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:35.560
 We're good for a while and then we drop the ball.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:38.360
 Like, you know, what happened to the apricots?

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:40.280
 You know, that kind of thing.

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:44.840
 You do have a pretty incredible group of people out here looking over to the farm.

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:45.820
 We do.

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:58.950
 And Mimi and I consistently say to ourselves and say to others that the greatest pleasure we have in farming is the community of people that we're farming with.

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:05.240
 Without exception, every one of them is quite wonderful and we're happy to see each day.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:08.340
 You're the perfect person to ask about this, I think.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:18.180
 Because most of my research has been in the ways that farmers, quite poor farmers use biodiversity to manage risk of going hungry for various reasons.

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:28.540
 Are there corollaries between, like, the way that you manage investment assets or how you thought about diversification in that way and how it works on the farmer?

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:31.880
 How strong does that corollary hold up?

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:45.450
 Yeah, well, I mean, in investing, you know, it's pretty formulaic and there's a spectrum of risk and you, depending on your risk profile, you'll load up at one end or the other.

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:53.780
 But generally, you cover the entire spectrum from bonds to high growth stocks to startup kind of situations.

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:58.650
 In farming, it's not, I wouldn't say it's high risk.

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:02.770
 It's simply the diversity is the same diversity you find in nature.

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:10.070
 When in a complex system, one thing, when it goes out of whack, it tends to be brought back into the system by the other elements in the system.

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:14.170
 So I said diversity was the enemy of profitability.

00:08:14.530 --> 00:08:24.080
 It's not the enemy of survival and what's, you know, the stability and integrity and beauty of biotic systems.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:25.680
 That's another metric.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:29.020
 And that's the metric that we're very happy to use.

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:34.670
 But we also have to play by the rules or nobody will pay attention to us.

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:37.010
 And so, you know, we work hard to be profitable.

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:40.180
 Let's talk a little bit about blackberries then.

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:42.700
 I was just hoping you'd bring that up.

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:49.500
 That's why I got curious in the first place that I started talking to you a little bit about blackberries and I realized that there is a passion.

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:55.740
 Sort of tap me on the shoulder and say, honey, not everybody cares that much about blackberries.

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:00.900
 When did you start caring about blackberries?

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:05.920
 I started a significant relationship with blackberries when I was about 11.

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:11.070
 And it was, you know, a youthful love that never left.

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:21.950
 And I noticed, in fact, when we're doing farm tours and I take people of any age into the blackberries, they immediately revert to being about 11 years old again.

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:30.830
 And there's just this sort of, I don't know what comes over you in a blackberry patch, but that's, it's something to do with being 11.

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:35.080
 Did you grow up in the Northwest by any chance?

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:35.360
 Yeah.

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:36.820
 I, well, my dad was in the military.

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:39.020
 We, I didn't grow up anywhere in particular.

00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:48.580
 Um, but I, when I was 11, we lived in Seattle and across from our house was a very large old sort of derelict property.

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:51.170
 Lucky for me that was covered with blackberries.

00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:56.130
 I grew up in, on Bainbridge Island and, um, yeah.

00:09:56.530 --> 00:10:00.870
 So my, my grandpa was also obsessed with fruit of all kinds.

00:10:01.010 --> 00:10:09.070
 And he would, we would devise elaborate ways of like backing the pickup truck into the blackberry ramble or setting up ladders on top of it.

00:10:09.370 --> 00:10:11.730
 Fairly dangerous ways of collecting buckets and buckets.

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:14.810
 The ladders is, that was my chosen technique.

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:18.410
 Drop a ladder over the top and walk over the top and get those good berries.

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:26.300
 You know, when you think about the question of blackberries, you could have asked that question in a different way.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:38.960
 And I, and I would have said, well, we grow blackberries because it's the perfect crop for a farm that wants to be, you know, a local farm.

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:44.040
 And the reason for that is that blackberries are a very fragile crop.

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:47.220
 They don't have to, well, depending on the varietal, not much shelf life.

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:53.660
 And if they can't get into the stores in a day, that's too long.

00:10:54.080 --> 00:11:01.630
 And that supply line is just impossible for any of the bigger outfits, no matter how sophisticated they are.

00:11:01.730 --> 00:11:08.650
 I mean, we grow, with our blueberries, we grow a lot of blueberries and we sell to Costco and, you know, big outfits.

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:15.300
 And we have coolers at the farm and we get our product into the stores as fast as anybody.

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:28.210
 But that still can be, you know, two or three days from the field through the processing to the semis through Costco's or Safeway's or Trader Joe's, you know, processing.

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:42.500
 Whereas at the farm, we pick on Friday afternoon and we're selling them at the Saturday market in Healdsburg or the San Rafael Civic Center market on Sunday, Berkeley, you know.

00:11:43.030 --> 00:11:47.490
 And what that translates to is that it's the only way you're going to get a good blackberry.

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:59.130
 I don't want to disparage any of the other blackberry growers, but you cannot find a good blackberry that's not coming from a local farm at a supermarket.

00:11:59.130 --> 00:12:05.660
 If it's from any of the big names in the berry business, the blackberries are not going to be good.

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:17.450
 When I talked to you a little bit about you're selling some blackberry plants and I thought a little bit about the idea of planting a blackberry kind of offended my Northwestern sensibilities.

00:12:17.450 --> 00:12:29.830
 So the difference is this, that when I was up in Oregon at our blueberry farm with our farming partner, Eric, and we're up on a hill overlooking the farm and there were some wild blackberries.

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:34.590
 And I went over to pick some blackberries and I said, hey, Eric, I know you love blackberries.

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:35.030
 You want some?

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:36.650
 And he went, no.

00:12:36.890 --> 00:12:38.090
 I said, you don't like blackberries?

00:12:38.410 --> 00:12:40.830
 He says, no, but I don't like those wild ones.

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:42.320
 And I went, what?

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:43.400
 He says, you'll see.

00:12:43.760 --> 00:13:00.340
 So we went up to, and I hadn't noticed it before, but Eric had planted out, oh, I think 75 different varietals in cooperation with the University of Arkansas, which they develop most of the commercial cultivars, blackberry cultivars in the country.

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:06.290
 And we grow them out as an educational thing in cooperation with them and OSU.

00:13:06.850 --> 00:13:12.030
 And he used to grow blackberries commercially, so he's the guy who sort of schooled me in this.

00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:14.860
 And he said, here, taste these, Peter.

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:17.140
 And he took them over to a varietal called Triple Crown.

00:13:18.050 --> 00:13:22.320
 And first of all, there was, you know, a 100-foot row of these plants.

00:13:22.540 --> 00:13:24.180
 They were heavy with berries.

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:25.540
 They were all ripe.

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:31.440
 It wasn't like you didn't have to scan and find that special fatty that was next to a bunch of green.

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:34.580
 They were all ripe at the same time, which for a grower is a big deal.

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:39.870
 They were, you know, twice or three times the size of any wild blackberry you've ever had.

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:46.200
 The flavor was so intense and blackberry jammy kind of flavor.

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:49.540
 And the seeds were minuscule.

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:55.270
 And right after that, I had the same conversion that Eric had.

00:13:55.370 --> 00:13:59.960
 I went, wild blackberries are wonderful, but, you know, I think I'll wait.

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:04.790
 Yeah, no, it really is an amazing flavor of those things.

00:14:04.890 --> 00:14:06.970
 A friend gave me some last summer.

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:10.050
 It's like, oh, this is why you would plant these kind of things.

00:14:10.270 --> 00:14:15.320
 Yeah, even in our patch, I planted three varietals.

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:19.460
 And there's one that I planted for commercial reasons.

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:23.260
 And that is that it's an erect type that doesn't take too much work.

00:14:23.420 --> 00:14:25.220
 But it's an early varietal.

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:30.920
 What I didn't know was it was supposed to taste, you know, supposed to have a good flavor characteristics.

00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:33.720
 It's not that great.

00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:35.340
 Probably take them out eventually.

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:38.930
 Kind of hard to pull out producing plants.

00:14:39.650 --> 00:14:48.360
 But what's interesting is one of our grocery store customers, they ask for that berry because it has better shelf life.

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:53.050
 It's less tender, which is probably why it doesn't taste as good as the other varietals.

00:14:53.570 --> 00:14:57.650
 But they prefer that one because they can keep it out longer.

00:14:57.950 --> 00:15:04.730
 And you just see the process of, you know, what happens to the quality of, I mean, that's just one product that I know something about.

00:15:05.090 --> 00:15:15.400
 But I think it's true probably across the board of products that you make compromises for the commercial reasons that detract from the, you know, the eating experience of the product.

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:27.960
 And the other thing I was just thinking again about monocultures is that on the farm, every single day, everything is pushing us towards simplification and ultimately a monoculture.

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:29.480
 I mean, we're not going there.

00:15:29.720 --> 00:15:41.800
 But, I mean, every crop requires pretty much specialized tools, you know, different care regimes, the agronomy regimes I mentioned, different amounts of labor.

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:49.020
 And in terms of profitability, with the blueberries, we have four or five tools that we use.

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:53.610
 I mean, they're big and they're complicated, but we don't need different kinds of sprayers.

00:15:53.750 --> 00:15:56.110
 We don't need different kinds of cultivators.

00:15:56.290 --> 00:15:58.510
 We don't need different kinds of pruning equipment.

00:15:58.890 --> 00:16:08.850
 You know, it's just a lot of one thing, you know, and especially when it comes to labor, you teach someone how to pick that one particular crop and they learn it.

00:16:08.850 --> 00:16:12.830
 You know, you've invested the time and then that time is amortized over the time that they're picking.

00:16:13.600 --> 00:16:22.560
 Here, it's like, you know, every crop has a certain way that you need to pick it if it's going to turn out to be well and then to process it, clean it, package it.

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:24.560
 Each one requires that.

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:28.740
 And every time we have meetings, there's this pressure to, like I said, to simplify.

00:16:29.950 --> 00:16:32.550
 And we so far are resisting that.

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:34.380
 We'll see.

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:35.860
 Come back in five years.

00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:38.200
 We'll be growing alfalfa.

00:16:40.250 --> 00:16:45.600
 Something tells me that alfalfa wouldn't be the crop that takes over in a land like this.

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:46.180
 It's a great crop.

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:47.420
 Alfalfa is pretty great.

00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:47.660
 Yeah.

00:16:48.060 --> 00:16:51.980
 You plant it and all you do is harvest for the next five years.

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:53.300
 It's right out the window.

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:55.540
 We do have about 10 acres of alfalfa.

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:58.740
 And it's really simple.

00:16:58.880 --> 00:16:59.620
 It's really easy.

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:02.560
 And it's good for the soil.

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:06.600
 You know, the roots are super deep, so it's good for the soil structure.

00:17:06.740 --> 00:17:09.880
 And, you know, it's a nitrogen-fixing crop as well.

00:17:11.370 --> 00:17:14.780
 What are the pressures that promote diversity on the farm?

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:16.400
 I know that you mentioned that.

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:19.140
 Nothing promotes diversity.

00:17:19.900 --> 00:17:21.540
 Everything promotes the opposite.

00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:27.790
 Labor, like I said, labor, particularly in Sonoma County, is a huge one.

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:28.770
 Yeah, sure.

00:17:28.810 --> 00:17:32.340
 And knowledgeable, skilled labor, you know, even more so.

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:35.380
 A friend is an anthropologist.

00:17:35.540 --> 00:17:39.600
 She writes about how high-diversity farming takes skilled labor.

00:17:40.510 --> 00:17:44.340
 And then monoculture takes less skilled labor.

00:17:44.620 --> 00:17:50.670
 And it's intertwined with the history of colonialism and exploited labor in that way.

00:17:51.330 --> 00:17:59.080
 It would be hard to exploit your labor force while growing a really diverse farm because it takes such skilled

00:17:59.080 --> 00:18:00.690
 and careful attention.

00:18:02.040 --> 00:18:02.440
 Yeah.

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:04.480
 I mean, I'm not sure what to say about that.

00:18:04.660 --> 00:18:12.440
 For example, on the blueberries, the fresh blueberry market, what you, you know, the berries you buy in a store,

00:18:12.560 --> 00:18:17.060
 are largely, there's exceptions, but 90% of those are hand-picked.

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:24.450
 And the crews that pick our berries, they start out, and they work for the same company.

00:18:24.950 --> 00:18:28.730
 One of my partners has a business called Homegrown Organics.

00:18:28.930 --> 00:18:32.870
 And these crews have worked for Tom for, I don't know, for over a decade at least.

00:18:33.450 --> 00:18:38.470
 And they follow the blueberry crop from outside San Diego all the way up to Oregon.

00:18:38.470 --> 00:18:45.070
 And they really like picking that crop because they're very good at that, so they make pretty good money.

00:18:45.610 --> 00:18:50.650
 I don't, I'm unaware of the exploitive aspect of that.

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:51.790
 I'm not sure where it is.

00:18:52.790 --> 00:18:55.440
 But it is, they're only doing one thing.

00:18:56.660 --> 00:19:01.220
 And they're doing it for economic necessity, of course, but, and they're good, you know.

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:06.320
 They would easily pick twice the volume that our guys can pick, you know.

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:13.900
 Yeah, I mean, I don't mean to say that that's the only way that monocultures can be run, but it would be, but that it's a...

00:19:13.900 --> 00:19:20.680
 I think what happens is monocultures tend, I mean, the attraction is more the specialized tools and the mechanization.

00:19:21.850 --> 00:19:24.600
 Imagine a specialized lettuce grower.

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:30.700
 If they're growing at a big enough scale, so by the way, it's not only monoculture, it's scale.

00:19:31.350 --> 00:19:36.140
 They can afford to use a robotic weeder.

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:40.180
 You know, basically a weeder that goes down and there's a camera.

00:19:40.650 --> 00:19:47.570
 And the machine or the camera interface can distinguish between a weed and lettuce and weed very close to very small plants.

00:19:48.050 --> 00:19:57.190
 That same weeding for us, you know, would be, I mean, you can't amortize the cost of a machine like that over an acre of lettuce.

00:19:57.350 --> 00:20:00.070
 You got to grow, you know, 200, 100.

00:20:00.210 --> 00:20:03.510
 I don't know what, I don't know how much they grow, but a lot of lettuce.

00:20:04.030 --> 00:20:05.890
 And the same is true of strawberry.

00:20:05.890 --> 00:20:10.590
 I mean, it just goes down the line, all the specialized equipment that small farms can't afford.

00:20:11.010 --> 00:20:11.510
 Sure, sure.

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:13.970
 Back to the blackberries.

00:20:14.190 --> 00:20:14.490
 Oh, yeah.

00:20:14.890 --> 00:20:17.940
 Have you been to Arkansas to this plot of it?

00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:26.940
 No, I mean, I'm not quite that eccentric, but I do, I realize I am drifting into eccentricville.

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:32.380
 At the market, the civic center market, I was standing in front.

00:20:32.380 --> 00:20:41.090
 And when people come by, you know, chatting with people, and I like to show them how to tell a ripe berry from a not ripe berry and things like that.

00:20:41.590 --> 00:20:43.690
 Or actually, produce in general.

00:20:43.810 --> 00:20:46.370
 It's fun to teach people about what to look, how to see.

00:20:47.150 --> 00:20:52.250
 And this woman came up and she was looking at the blackberries and I said,

00:20:52.430 --> 00:20:57.140
 Oh, are you interested to know how to tell what's ripe and what's not ripe?

00:20:57.200 --> 00:21:01.390
 And she went, Oh, oh, you're the blackberry guy.

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:03.370
 I went, the blackberry?

00:21:03.490 --> 00:21:03.910
 Oh, no.

00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:05.870
 I've become the blackberry guy.

00:21:08.010 --> 00:21:09.190
 Do you have a reputation?

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:10.990
 I'm afraid so.

00:21:12.170 --> 00:21:16.310
 Now I started to grow, just for fun, these giant pumpkins.

00:21:16.310 --> 00:21:25.110
 And I'm afraid that's going to lead down the same path of, uh-oh, he's getting stranger and stranger every decade.

00:21:26.290 --> 00:21:29.870
 My mom has an eccentric collection of rare fruit trees.

00:21:30.530 --> 00:21:38.720
 And, um, so I grew up going to a lot of rare fruit tree society get-togethers where exchanging scion woods and that kind of thing.

00:21:39.100 --> 00:21:40.500
 It's a, it's a good crowd.

00:21:40.780 --> 00:21:42.100
 I mean, like, I don't know.

00:21:42.440 --> 00:21:44.940
 I'd lump them in with, like, the mushroom people.

00:21:45.620 --> 00:21:47.880
 Also, like, a kind of a quality group of people.

00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:53.640
 We, we, we have one of those mycophile friends, Kevin.

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:56.480
 And he, and, uh, he's amazing.

00:21:56.740 --> 00:21:58.560
 You know, I, I, I agree with you.

00:21:59.140 --> 00:22:04.940
 Although, the mushrooms, they're, they're, I can imagine, there's a lot of explanations for why that crew's so eccentric.

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:10.740
 Are, are the blackberries producing yet?

00:22:10.980 --> 00:22:11.800
 Are they starting to get white?

00:22:12.220 --> 00:22:12.620
 Yes.

00:22:12.920 --> 00:22:15.120
 Blackberry season started a few days ago.

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:21.440
 So we, we have, like most growers, we have different varietals that fruit early, mid, late season.

00:22:21.440 --> 00:22:23.570
 So we have a constant, uh, product.

00:22:23.690 --> 00:22:28.930
 And the blackberries have been, as I mentioned earlier, they've been developed so that they fruit.

00:22:28.930 --> 00:22:33.460
 Within a narrow window of time, you know, a two to four week window of time.

00:22:34.010 --> 00:22:38.740
 Um, so we have, we have an early variety that actually isn't fruiting yet.

00:22:38.860 --> 00:22:42.510
 And we have a mid-season variety that's really the best one.

00:22:42.950 --> 00:22:44.730
 And it's not fruiting yet.

00:22:45.070 --> 00:22:50.540
 But I realized that there's a variety we grow, um, called Primark.

00:22:50.740 --> 00:22:56.260
 For you folks at home that want to have a, no, which, no secrets here.

00:22:56.500 --> 00:23:00.000
 Um, and, uh, Primark is a, is different from the other ones.

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:06.430
 It, it doesn't fruit as heavy, it, but it fruits over from the beginning to the end of the season.

00:23:06.430 --> 00:23:08.150
 So it'll fruit all the way into October.

00:23:08.570 --> 00:23:12.630
 And it slows down and it's hardly enough for picking.

00:23:12.870 --> 00:23:14.550
 So we, we have to invest too much labor.

00:23:14.650 --> 00:23:19.770
 So we stopped picking for the market, but it's there for visitors to the farm to pick.

00:23:19.830 --> 00:23:21.870
 And it makes plenty of sense if you're going to make a pie.

00:23:22.430 --> 00:23:29.190
 Um, and those berries, I noticed a few days ago, down low, low on the bush, kind of hidden from view.

